1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,900
Diana: So, Tim, we have to do a short summary of our work experience on a farm.

2
00:00:05,900 --> 00:00:10,655
Tim: Right. My farm was great, but arranging the work experience was hard.

3
00:00:10,655 --> 00:00:15,044
One problem was it was miles away and I don't drive.

4
00:00:15,044 --> 00:00:19,300
And also, I'd really wanted a placement for a month

5
00:00:19,300 --> 00:00:22,360
but I could only get one for two weeks.

6
00:00:22,360 --> 00:00:28,422
Diana: I was lucky, the farmer let me stay on the farm so I didn't have to travel.

7
00:00:28,422 --> 00:00:32,570
But finding the right sort of farm to apply to wasn't easy.

8
00:00:32,570 --> 00:00:35,430
Tim: No, they don't seem to have websites, do they.

9
00:00:35,430 --> 00:00:39,330
I found mine through a friend of my mother's, but it wasn't easy.

10
00:00:39,330 --> 00:00:40,720
Diana: No.

11
00:00:40,720 --> 00:00:43,614
Tim: My farm was mostly livestock, especially sheep.

12
00:00:43,614 --> 00:00:47,040
I really enjoyed helping out with them

13
00:00:47,580 --> 00:00:50,850
I was up most of one night helping a sheep deliver a lamb...

14
00:00:50,850 --> 00:00:51,850
Diana: On your own?

15
00:00:51,850 --> 00:00:56,204
Tim: No, the farmer was there, and he told me what to do.

16
00:00:56,204 --> 00:00:59,218
It wasn't a straightforward birth, but I managed.

17
00:00:59,218 --> 00:01:03,280
It was a great feeling to see the lamb stagger to its feet

18
00:01:03,280 --> 00:01:05,246
and start feeding almost straightaway,

19
00:01:05,246 --> 00:01:07,590
and to know that it was OK.

20
00:01:07,590 --> 00:01:08,390
Diana: Mm.

21
00:01:08,390 --> 00:01:11,537
Tim: Then another time a lamb had broken its leg,

22
00:01:11,537 --> 00:01:14,360
and they got the vet in to set it

23
00:01:14,640 --> 00:01:18,460
and he talked me through what he was doing. That was really useful.

24
00:01:18,460 --> 00:01:20,910
Diana: Yes, my farm had sheep too.

25
00:01:20,910 --> 00:01:25,810
The farm was in a valley and they had a lowland breed called Suffolks,

26
00:01:25,810 --> 00:01:30,010
although the farmer said they'd had other breeds in the past.

27
00:01:30,010 --> 00:01:32,320
Tim: So were they bred for their meat?

28
00:01:32,320 --> 00:01:36,260
Diana: Mostly, yes. They're quite big and solid.

29
00:01:36,260 --> 00:01:38,060
Tim: My farm was up in the hills

30
00:01:38,060 --> 00:01:41,560
and they had a different breed of sheep, they were Cheviots.

31
00:01:41,560 --> 00:01:45,260
Diana: Oh, I heard their wool's really sought after.

32
00:01:45,260 --> 00:01:48,560
Tim: Yes. It's very hardwearing and they use it for carpets.

33
00:01:48,560 --> 00:01:49,500
Diana: Right.

34
00:01:49,500 --> 00:01:54,811
Tim: I was interested in the amount of supplements they add to animals' feed nowadays.

35
00:01:54,811 --> 00:01:59,060
Like, even the chickens got extra vitamins and electrolytes in their feed.

36
00:01:59,060 --> 00:02:02,805
Diana: Yes, I found that too. And they're not cheap.

37
00:02:02,805 --> 00:02:07,360
But my farmer said some are overpriced for what they are

38
00:02:07,360 --> 00:02:10,295
And he didn't give them as a matter of routine,

39
00:02:10,295 --> 00:02:14,040
just at times when the chickens seemed to particularly require them.

40
00:02:14,040 --> 00:02:15,900
Tim: Yes, mine said the same

41
00:02:16,100 --> 00:02:20,525
He said certain breeds of chickens might need more supplements than the others,

42
00:02:20,525 --> 00:02:24,280
but the cheap and expensive ones are all basically the same.

43
00:02:24,280 --> 00:02:25,090
Diana: Mm.

44
00:02:25,090 --> 00:02:28,890
Tim: So did your farm have any other livestock, Diana?

45
00:02:28,890 --> 00:02:34,700
Diana: Yes, dairy cows. I made a really embarrassing mistake

46
00:02:34,700 --> 00:02:35,663
when I was working in the milk shed.

47
00:02:35,663 --> 00:02:38,297
Some cows had been treated with antibiotics,

48
00:02:38,297 --> 00:02:43,940
so their milk wasn't suitable for human consumption

49
00:02:43,980 --> 00:02:45,447
and it had to be put in a separate container.

50
00:02:45,447 --> 00:02:49,260
But I got mixed up

51
00:02:49,280 --> 00:02:52,973
and I poured some milk from the wrong cow in with the milk for humans,

52
00:02:52,973 --> 00:02:59,370
so the whole lot had to be thrown away. The farmer wasn't too happy with me.

53
00:02:59,370 --> 00:03:05,087
Tim: I asked my farmer how much he depended on the vet to deal with health problems.

54
00:03:05,087 --> 00:03:08,540
I'd read reports that the livestock's health is being affected

55
00:03:08,860 --> 00:03:11,812
as farmers are under pressure to increase production.

56
00:03:11,812 --> 00:03:14,380
Well, he didn't agree with that

57
00:03:14,460 --> 00:03:18,201
but he said that actually some of the stuff the vets do,

58
00:03:18,201 --> 00:03:21,900
like minor operations, he'd be quite capable of doing himself.

59
00:03:21,900 --> 00:03:32,250
Diana: Yeah. My farmer said the same. But he reckons vets' skills are still needed.

60
00:03:32,250 --> 00:03:39,074
Diana: Now we've got to give a bit of feedback about last term's modules - just short comments, apparently.

61
00:03:39,074 --> 00:03:40,780
Shall we do that now?

62
00:03:40,780 --> 00:03:43,830
Tim: OK. So medical terminology.

63
00:03:43,830 --> 00:03:47,180
Diana: Well, my heart sank when I saw that

64
00:03:47,340 --> 00:03:50,608
especially right at the beginning of the course.

65
00:03:50,608 --> 00:03:53,000
And I did struggle with it.

66
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:58,273
Tim: I'd thought it'd be hard, but actually I found it all quite straightforward.

67
00:03:58,273 --> 00:04:00,910
What did you think about diet and nutrition?

68
00:04:00,910 --> 00:04:02,720
Diana: OK, I suppose.

69
00:04:02,720 --> 00:04:05,900
Tim: Do you remember what they told us about pet food

70
00:04:05,900 --> 00:04:08,380
and the fact that there's such limited checking into

71
00:04:08,380 --> 00:04:10,525
whether or not it's contaminated?

72
00:04:10,525 --> 00:04:13,540
I mean in comparison with the checks on food for humans

73
00:04:13,660 --> 00:04:15,150
I thought that was terrible.

74
00:04:15,150 --> 00:04:21,131
Diana: Mm. I think the module that really impressed me was the animal disease one,

75
00:04:21,131 --> 00:04:25,916
when we looked at domesticated animals in different parts of the world,

76
00:04:25,916 --> 00:04:28,960
like camels and water buffalo and alpaca

77
00:04:29,760 --> 00:04:32,295
The economies of so many countries depend on these,

78
00:04:32,295 --> 00:04:37,080
but scientists don't know much about the diseases that affect them.

79
00:04:37,080 --> 00:04:42,770
Tim: Yes, I thought they'd know a lot about ways of controlling and eradicating those diseases,

80
00:04:42,770 --> 00:04:45,448
but that's not the case at all.

81
00:04:45,448 --> 00:04:47,456
I loved the wildlife medication unit.

82
00:04:47,456 --> 00:04:51,138
Things like helping birds that have been caught in oil spills.

83
00:04:51,138 --> 00:04:54,150
That's something I hadn't thought about before.

84
00:04:54,150 --> 00:04:58,980
Diana: Yeah, I thought I might write my dissertation on something connected with that.
